<h4 class="titles">General Description of the Course</h4>
<p class="leftindent">As one of the most active areas of research in mathematics, the theory of Lie algebras is a branch of algebra with close connections to several other advanced areas of mathematics such as harmonic analysis, differential geometry, number theory, cryptography and combinatorics. Lie algebras have a broad range of applications in other disciplines, including quantum physics and computer science.</p>

<p class="leftindent"> This course serves as an introduction to the most fundamental concepts of Lie algebras. Undergraduate students interested in working on summer research projects or writing an Honours/M.Sc. thesis
with one of the members of the department's <a href"http://mysite.science.uottawa.ca/algebra//index.html">Algebra, Lie Theory, and Representation Theory Groups</a> will find this course very useful and interesting.  </p>

<h4 class="titles">Official Course Description</h4>

<p class="leftindent">Structure of solvable, nilpotent and semisimple finite dimensional Lie algebras.
</p>

<h4 class="titles">Prerequisites</h4>

<p class="leftindent">MAT3141
</p>

<h4 class="titles">Course Text</h4>

<p class="leftindent">
The lectures are essentially based on the following text:
<ul>
<li>
Karin Erdmann, Mark J. Wildon, <i>Introduction to Lie Algebras</i>, Springer,  2006.
</li>
</ul>
</p>
<p class="leftindent">This book is the main text for students enrolled in either MAT4142 or MAT5143. 
You can purchase it at the 
<a target="_top" href="http://www.bkstr.com/webapp/wcs/stores/servlet/StoreCatalogDisplay?storeId=11012">
University of Ottawa Bookstore</a>. You can also 
download it
from <a href="http://www.springerlink.com/content/978-1-84628-040-5/contents/">
the publisher's website</a> when you are connected to the internet on campus. (Note that you should download each chapter separately.) 
</p>
